2. Prepare the soil: Use potting soil specifically designed for indoor gardens or make your own using compost, sand, and perlite. Add a few drops of water soluble fertilizer to the soil before planting.

3. Plant your seeds: Follow the instructions on the seed packets for planting depth and spacing. Some vegetables like tomatoes, peppers, and cucumbers can be planted directly into the soil, while others may need to be transplanted indoors.

4. Water regularly: Indoor plants require less water than outdoor plants, so make sure to water them only when they're dry. Overwatering can lead to root rot and other issues.

5. Keep pests away: Indoor plants are prone to pests like spider mites and mealybugs. Use a good quality insecticidal soap or neem oil spray to control these pesky creatures.

