There are approximately 10,000 thunderstorms per year in the United States alone, and each of these storms can produce up to 20-30 lightning strikes. Lightning is a natural phenomenon that occurs when an electric charge from one cloud reaches another cloud or ground, causing a spark that ignites a fireball. The energy released by lightning can cause damage to buildings, power lines, and other electrical equipment. In addition, lightning can also cause fires and explosions if it strikes nearby objects such as trees or buildings. Therefore, there are more than enough lightning strikes occurring on Earth every day for us to be aware of them.

